{
  "concepts": [
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "An index of concepts defined by a member outside every bounded context that member declares. The source context is the owning member; an index row declares no bounded context.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":335,
          "quote":"This section indexes the concepts that a member defines outside every\nbounded context it declares. The source context of each is its owning\nmember\n([FOUNDATIONS §2 Imports between members](FOUNDATIONS.md#imports-between-members)),\nwhich the second column names in place of a bounded-context identity. A\nrow here declares no context; a context holds one of these concepts only\nby its own import, as §8 records for the concepts that REFERENCE_FRAME §1\nimports (§2; P1, P3).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/GLOSSARY.md#GLOSSARY%2F16.%20Concepts%20of%20members%20without%20a%20declared%20context",
          "start_line":328
        }
      ],
      "key":"member_concept_index",
      "label":"Concepts outside declared bounded contexts"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "FOUNDATIONS-owned indexed concepts: recovered, independent, derived and hypothesis, owned by Vocabulary of standing and linked to Commitments; algorithmic description, informational interpretation and computational realisation as accounts of a construction, linked to SPEC_METHOD Build-tenant binding; Import, Import Declaration, Adoption and Adoption Premise, owned by Imports between members and linked to PRODUCT_DEFINITION semantic resolution, PROFILES boundary and REFERENCE_FRAME imports; Material Family, Family Table, Instance Premise and Added Choice, owned by Foundation to law with no relation clause listed; Adopting Product, owned there and linked to Results; Supporting Evidence of a categorical claim, owned by the Algebra formal fragment and linked to EVALUATION conformance; Join in four senses—categorical join, composition boundary, join of results and relation join—owned by Vocabulary of standing and linked to CONFORMANCE counterexample cases; Assurance Relations, Assertion Provenance, Execution Provenance, Observation Sufficiency, Impact Cover, Validity Dependency and Mandatory Gate, owned by Assurance relations and linked to ACTIVATION closed-result reuse, EVALUATION claims, ASSURANCE test authority and RELEASE acceptance. These are locators, not supplied full definitions.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":346,
          "quote":"| `Assurance Relations`; `Assertion Provenance`; `Execution Provenance`; `Observation Sufficiency`; `Impact Cover`; `Validity Dependency`; `Mandatory Gate` | FOUNDATIONS | [`FOUNDATIONS.md#assurance-relations`](FOUNDATIONS.md#assurance-relations) | [`ACTIVATION.md#7-closed-result-and-reuse`](ACTIVATION.md#7-closed-result-and-reuse); [`EVALUATION.md#1-claims`](EVALUATION.md#1-claims); [`ASSURANCE.md#3-test-authority`](ASSURANCE.md#3-test-authority); [`RELEASE.md#8-acceptance`](RELEASE.md#8-acceptance) |",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/GLOSSARY.md#GLOSSARY%2F16.%20Concepts%20of%20members%20without%20a%20declared%20context",
          "start_line":346
        },
        {
          "end_line":345,
          "quote":"| `Join` (four senses: categorical join; composition boundary; join of results; relation join) | FOUNDATIONS | [`FOUNDATIONS.md#1-vocabulary-of-standing`](FOUNDATIONS.md#1-vocabulary-of-standing) | [`CONFORMANCE.md#1-counterexample-cases`](CONFORMANCE.md#1-counterexample-cases) |",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/GLOSSARY.md#GLOSSARY%2F16.%20Concepts%20of%20members%20without%20a%20declared%20context",
          "start_line":345
        }
      ],
      "key":"foundations_indexed_concepts",
      "label":"FOUNDATIONS concept inventory"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "ACTIVATION-owned indexed concepts: Current State, Destination and Transition at Transitions, linked to Work state and restart; Construction Relation, Strategy, Composition Boundary, Feedback and Diagnosis and the four causes at Construction relation, linked to SPEC_METHOD intake triage, EVALUATION claims and FOUNDATIONS construction synthesis; Configuration at Configuration, linked to FOUNDATIONS frame objects and RF-009; Acquisition, Derived Surface and deciding owner section at Context construction under an envelope, with no relation clause listed; Rewrite Step, Licensing Law, Exact Step, Approximate Step, Adequacy Relation and End-to-End Bound at Refinement, with no relation clause listed; Truth Surface and actor's account at The workspace and an actor's account, linked to Transitions.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":352,
          "quote":"| `Truth Surface`; actor's account | ACTIVATION | [`ACTIVATION.md#the-workspace-and-an-actors-account`](ACTIVATION.md#the-workspace-and-an-actors-account) | [`ACTIVATION.md#transitions`](ACTIVATION.md#transitions) |",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/GLOSSARY.md#GLOSSARY%2F16.%20Concepts%20of%20members%20without%20a%20declared%20context",
          "start_line":352
        },
        {
          "end_line":351,
          "quote":"| `Rewrite Step`; `Licensing Law`; `Exact Step`; `Approximate Step`; `Adequacy Relation`; `End-to-End Bound` | ACTIVATION | [`ACTIVATION.md#refinement`](ACTIVATION.md#refinement) | none |",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/GLOSSARY.md#GLOSSARY%2F16.%20Concepts%20of%20members%20without%20a%20declared%20context",
          "start_line":351
        }
      ],
      "key":"activation_indexed_concepts",
      "label":"ACTIVATION concept inventory"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "EVALUATION owns Equivalence Ladder, semantic equivalence of realisations, Computational Equivalence and Categorical Equivalence at Conformance of realisations, linked to FOUNDATIONS Algebra; Semantic Equivalence of concepts in glossary §5 is explicitly another concept. EVALUATION also owns Adequate Attention and Actual-Use Evidence at Frame qualification, linked to ACTIVATION context construction. CONFORMANCE owns Discriminator and Common Discriminators at Conformance, linked to Discriminator coverage, and Logical-Review Check at Discriminator coverage, linked to EVALUATION review results. PROFILES owns the profile import rule at The boundary, linked to FOUNDATIONS imports.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":357,
          "quote":"| `Equivalence Ladder`; semantic equivalence of realisations; `Computational Equivalence`; `Categorical Equivalence` | EVALUATION | [`EVALUATION.md#6-conformance-of-realisations`](EVALUATION.md#6-conformance-of-realisations) | [`FOUNDATIONS.md#4-algebra`](FOUNDATIONS.md#4-algebra); `Semantic Equivalence` of concepts in §5 is another concept |\n| `Adequate Attention`; `Actual-Use Evidence` | EVALUATION | [`EVALUATION.md#frame-qualification`](EVALUATION.md#frame-qualification) | [`ACTIVATION.md#2-context-construction-under-an-envelope`](ACTIVATION.md#2-context-construction-under-an-envelope) |\n| `Discriminator`; `Common Discriminators` | CONFORMANCE | [`CONFORMANCE.md#conformance`](CONFORMANCE.md#conformance) | [`CONFORMANCE.md#5-discriminator-coverage`](CONFORMANCE.md#5-discriminator-coverage) |\n| `Logical-Review Check` | CONFORMANCE | [`CONFORMANCE.md#5-discriminator-coverage`](CONFORMANCE.md#5-discriminator-coverage) | [`EVALUATION.md#4-review-results`](EVALUATION.md#4-review-results) |\n| profile import rule | PROFILES | [`PROFILES.md#1-the-boundary`](PROFILES.md#1-the-boundary) | [`FOUNDATIONS.md#imports-between-members`](FOUNDATIONS.md#imports-between-members) |",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/GLOSSARY.md#GLOSSARY%2F16.%20Concepts%20of%20members%20without%20a%20declared%20context",
          "start_line":353
        }
      ],
      "key":"assessment_and_profile_indexed_concepts",
      "label":"Assessment and profile concept inventory"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Separate indexed result domains: DESIGN_MODULE cross-view axiom verdict has pass, fail and not_applicable and relates to FOUNDATIONS Results; DESIGN_MODULE ontology and design verdicts use accepted at Gate and retrospective work and relate to ACTIVATION Disposition. UX Results of this profile owns four distinct domains, each linked to FOUNDATIONS Results: surface review {compliant, violation, design_module_incomplete}; replay proof {replay_passes, replay_fails}; closure obligations {closure_obligations_met, closure_unproven}; carrier admission {carrier_admitted, carrier_missing}. No evaluation is performed by listing them.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":363,
          "quote":"| axiom verdict: `pass`; `fail`; `not_applicable` | DESIGN_MODULE | [`DESIGN_MODULE.md#cross-view-axiom-evaluation`](DESIGN_MODULE.md#cross-view-axiom-evaluation) | [`FOUNDATIONS.md#results`](FOUNDATIONS.md#results) |\n| Ontology verdict and design verdict: `accepted` | DESIGN_MODULE | [`DESIGN_MODULE.md#gate-and-retrospective-work`](DESIGN_MODULE.md#gate-and-retrospective-work) | [`ACTIVATION.md#disposition`](ACTIVATION.md#disposition) |\n| surface review: `compliant`; `violation`; `design_module_incomplete` | UX | [`UX.md#15-results-of-this-profile`](UX.md#15-results-of-this-profile) | [`FOUNDATIONS.md#results`](FOUNDATIONS.md#results) |\n| replay proof: `replay_passes`; `replay_fails` | UX | [`UX.md#15-results-of-this-profile`](UX.md#15-results-of-this-profile) | [`FOUNDATIONS.md#results`](FOUNDATIONS.md#results) |\n| closure obligations: `closure_obligations_met`; `closure_unproven` | UX | [`UX.md#15-results-of-this-profile`](UX.md#15-results-of-this-profile) | [`FOUNDATIONS.md#results`](FOUNDATIONS.md#results) |\n| carrier admission: `carrier_admitted`; `carrier_missing` | UX | [`UX.md#15-results-of-this-profile`](UX.md#15-results-of-this-profile) | [`FOUNDATIONS.md#results`](FOUNDATIONS.md#results) |",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/GLOSSARY.md#GLOSSARY%2F16.%20Concepts%20of%20members%20without%20a%20declared%20context",
          "start_line":358
        }
      ],
      "key":"indexed_verdict_domains",
      "label":"Indexed design and UX verdict domains"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A sense locator rather than a definition catalogue. indeterminate: FOUNDATIONS Results core value when admissible evidence cannot decide; EVALUATION Result and triage projection payload class, not a result. not_applicable: EVALUATION triage class, not a result; RELEASE §9 successor disposition with bounded reason; SPEC_METHOD lifecycle phase declared inapplicable with reason; DESIGN_MODULE head/state-model and ACTIVATION §4 design-grounded exclusion/entity-lifecycle matrix entry; TRAVERSAL_OCCURRENCE judgment value. refusal/refused: REFERENCE_FRAME §5 method-function activation or translation refusal; AC-007 a_c constraint or basis refusal; TRAVERSAL_OCCURRENCE judgment decision and stop; ACTIVATION admission refusal of a work contract. gap: AC-007 stop with a core value; FOUNDATIONS conjunction gap without its own value. disposition: ACTIVATION boundary acceptance; RELEASE predecessor-claim successor disposition; TICKETS obligation repricing; PROJECT_REFERENCE_FRAME_BASIS_TEMPLATE Coverage And Residuals planning choice for an evaluation, not coverage status. missing: absence of a closed result, FOUNDATIONS Results/Not performed. adoption: PRODUCT_DEFINITION §7 adoption of an available profile; SPEC_METHOD §6 shared method lesson adoption; RELEASE §4 consumer release-basis adoption. Closed/closed: POSTING_GUIDE §7 discussion state; TICKETS §4 ticket or sprint status.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":371,
          "quote":"Some labels name different things in different clauses. Each row below\nlocates one sense with the clause that owns it; the label's meaning at an\noccurrence is the sense its owning clause gives, and the occurrence\nqualifies the label or states its predicate where it is used. Equal\nspelling across these rows establishes no equivalence (§1; P1, P3).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/GLOSSARY.md#GLOSSARY%2F17.%20Labels%20with%20several%20senses",
          "start_line":367
        },
        {
          "end_line":394,
          "quote":"| disposition | a planning choice for an evaluation in a basis inventory, not a coverage status | `templates/PROJECT_REFERENCE_FRAME_BASIS_TEMPLATE.md`, Coverage And Residuals |",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/GLOSSARY.md#GLOSSARY%2F17.%20Labels%20with%20several%20senses",
          "start_line":394
        }
      ],
      "key":"clause_owned_label_senses",
      "label":"Clause-owned senses of shared labels"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Tier 1 member owning identity law: scoped uniqueness, type, stability, readable/opaque forms, hierarchy, engine/domain split, authority conservation, default-format overrides and publication, parsing, object/release boundary, minimum questions, examples and implementation consequences. It consumes FOUNDATIONS §2 Object, Authority and Basis without redefining them. AC-002 applies scoped identity to every calculus identity with a coordinate map; calculus-edition identity remains owned by AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS §8 and release names/forms by RELEASE. Clauses name their requiring P0–P6 principles; identity is P3's commitment and authority P6's.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":23,
          "quote":"identity is P3's commitment and authority P6's.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/IDENTITY.md#IDENTITY",
          "start_line":23
        },
        {
          "end_line":13,
          "quote":"Object (a subject with scoped identity of authority, kind and value),\nAuthority with its kinds, and Basis are defined once, and adds no\ndefinition to tier 1.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/IDENTITY.md#IDENTITY",
          "start_line":11
        }
      ],
      "key":"identity_member",
      "label":"IDENTITY member"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Object identity is always scoped and typed, unique in its lawful scope and stable enough for its governed purpose. Its minimum semantic model is authority + kind + value, through which identity should be interpretable. It is not a universal naked-string convention.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":105,
          "quote":"identity = authority + kind + value",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/IDENTITY.md#IDENTITY%2F2.%20Core%20law%2FScoped%20identity",
          "start_line":105
        },
        {
          "end_line":29,
          "quote":"Identity is scoped, typed, unique, and stable enough for the governed\npurpose. Identity is not a flat global string convention; it is made\nunique by hierarchy and type (P3).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/IDENTITY.md#IDENTITY%2F1.%20Position",
          "start_line":27
        }
      ],
      "key":"scoped_identity",
      "label":"Scoped object identity"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A useful identity hierarchy is authority / kind / value / revision. Examples distinguish product-authority requirement REQ-F-CAPABILITY-001, product-authority requirement_family REQ-F-CAPABILITY, runtime-authority run UUID and release-authority release 1.0.0-rc.1. Family/member and runtime/domain identities remain different semantic kinds even when serialised as strings.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":201,
          "quote":"The important point is that `REQ-F-CAPABILITY` and `REQ-F-CAPABILITY-001`\nare not the same kind of identity, and that a runtime `run_id` and a\ndomain requirement identity do not belong to the same semantic category\neven if both serialise as strings (P3).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/IDENTITY.md#IDENTITY%2F4.%20Identity%20hierarchy",
          "start_line":198
        }
      ],
      "key":"identity_hierarchy",
      "label":"Identity hierarchy"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "An object's boundary states what belongs to it and what its identity does not depend on. It is an evidence- and basis-bounded claim with a proof obligation, not something established by storage, schema or name.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":151,
          "quote":"An object's boundary, what belongs to the object and what its identity\ndoes not depend on, is a claim with a proof obligation\n([FOUNDATIONS §3](FOUNDATIONS.md#3-commitments), P3), not a property that\nits storage, schema or name establishes. A boundary claim names its\nobject, the evidence that supports it and the basis it holds on, and it\nholds only as far as that evidence goes (P3, P6).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/IDENTITY.md#IDENTITY%2F2.%20Core%20law%2FBoundaries%20and%20sufficiency%20claims",
          "start_line":146
        }
      ],
      "key":"object_boundary_claim",
      "label":"Object boundary claim"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Material sufficiency under RF-003 is a claim that observed boundary state suffices for a particular task. Statistical Markov boundary is the stronger hypothesis that the boundary screens the interior from everything outside it. Task sufficiency establishes neither completeness nor that stronger claim.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":160,
          "quote":"A claim that an\nobject's observed boundary state is sufficient for a task is a material\nsufficiency claim of\n[RF-003](REFERENCE_FRAME.md#rf-003-material-sufficiency), bounded by\nthat task: it is not completeness, and it does not establish that the\nboundary screens the object's interior from everything outside it. That\nstronger claim, a statistical Markov boundary, is a hypothesis\n([FOUNDATIONS §5](FOUNDATIONS.md#5-hypotheses)); no work relies on it as\nestablished, and a profile that uses such a construct states its\nepistemic status (P5).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/IDENTITY.md#IDENTITY%2F2.%20Core%20law%2FBoundaries%20and%20sufficiency%20claims",
          "start_line":151
        }
      ],
      "key":"boundary_sufficiency_and_markov_hypothesis",
      "label":"Task sufficiency and Markov hypothesis"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Identity for runtime-owned objects, including runs, function calls, jobs, execution frames, continuations and events. The substrate/runtime may provide defaults, opaque forms and engine-local generation rules.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":210,
          "quote":"The substrate or runtime may define default identities for runtime-owned\nobjects, such as runs, function calls, jobs, execution frames,\ncontinuations and events. For these, the engine may use opaque identities and engine-local\ngeneration rules.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/IDENTITY.md#IDENTITY%2F5.%20Authority%20split%2FEngine-owned%20identity",
          "start_line":207
        }
      ],
      "key":"engine_owned_identity",
      "label":"Engine-owned identity"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Derived domains own semantic identity grammar for their constitutional objects, including requirements, requirement families, acceptance criteria, scenarios, ADRs/design decisions and published domain assets. The domain defines kinds, distinctions, uniqueness and parsing/reading.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":221,
          "quote":"Derived domains own the semantic identity grammar for their own\nconstitutional objects, such as requirements, requirement families,\nacceptance criteria, scenarios, ADRs or design decisions, and published\ndomain assets. The domain defines which identity kinds exist, how they\nare distinguished, what makes them unique, and how they are parsed or\nread.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/IDENTITY.md#IDENTITY%2F5.%20Authority%20split%2FDomain-owned%20identity",
          "start_line":216
        }
      ],
      "key":"domain_owned_identity",
      "label":"Domain-owned identity"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Authority is an identity-bearing semantic relation preserving owner, kind, governed subject, scope and governing basis. It is not a display label, capability string, actor identity or reachable code path. FOUNDATIONS §2 owns Authority and its kinds.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":230,
          "quote":"Authority is an identity-bearing semantic relation, not a display label,\ncapability string, actor identity or reachable code path. Its identity\npreserves owner, kind, governed subject, scope and governing basis (P6,\nP3; the Authority\nobject and its kinds are FOUNDATIONS §2).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/IDENTITY.md#IDENTITY%2F6.%20Authority%20identity%20and%20conservation%20%28STDO-UP-004%29",
          "start_line":226
        }
      ],
      "key":"authority_identity_relation",
      "label":"Authority identity"
    },
    {
      "conditions": ["Human authority acts through an explicitly admitted bounded proxy."],
      "definition": "A proxy grant names grantor, actor, subject, scope, permitted and excluded decisions, validity, delegation rights, revocation and provenance. Proxy actor identity remains distinct from human-authority identity.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":245,
          "quote":"Human authority may act directly or through an explicitly admitted\nbounded proxy. Proxy actor identity and human-authority identity remain\ndistinct; a profile's own surfaces may project the human-authority\nrelation under that profile's term (PROFILES). A proxy grant names its\ngrantor, actor, subject, scope, permitted and excluded decisions,\nvalidity, delegation rights, revocation and provenance (P6, P3).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/IDENTITY.md#IDENTITY%2F6.%20Authority%20identity%20and%20conservation%20%28STDO-UP-004%29",
          "start_line":240
        }
      ],
      "key":"bounded_proxy_grant",
      "label":"Bounded proxy grant"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A derived domain's published replacement of format or structure defaults supplied by its bound runtime realisation for the domain's own kinds. Defaults are starter conventions and convenience contracts; the realisation's actual default surfaces belong to DESIGN_MODULE §12 realisation law.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":270,
          "quote":"Default identity formats that a bound runtime realisation publishes are\nstarter conventions and convenience contracts, not universal doctrine;\nthey are lawful only so long as derived domains may override them (P3,\nP6). What such a realisation publishes, and for which surfaces, is\nrealisation law outside this member\n([DESIGN_MODULE §12](DESIGN_MODULE.md#identity-defaults-from-a-bound-runtime-realisation)).\n\nDerived domains may override identity format and structure for their own\nkinds.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/IDENTITY.md#IDENTITY%2F7.%20Default%20formats%20and%20overrides%2FDerived%20domain%20overrides",
          "start_line":262
        }
      ],
      "key":"domain_identity_override",
      "label":"Domain identity override"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "An identity reader/parser operating under published identity law. Regex over prose is at most a provisional extractor over a surface with identity law defined elsewhere; it is not a complete identity model.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":314,
          "quote":"Regex extraction from prose is not a complete identity model. At most,\nregex may be a provisional extractor over a surface whose identity law is\nalready defined elsewhere (P3, P1).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/IDENTITY.md#IDENTITY%2F8.%20Parsing",
          "start_line":312
        }
      ],
      "key":"identity_reader",
      "label":"Identity parsing"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Distinct coordinates: object identity, release identity, publication/revision coordinates and mutable discovery selectors. An immutable release cut is the only exact operative release identifier; a mutable version-line selector names only the highest-ordinal published immutable cut on its line. Release version is a release-process fact, not the live constitutional identity of every project object.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":345,
          "quote":"- an immutable release cut is the only exact operative release\n  identifier;\n- the mutable version-line selector only names the highest-ordinal\n  published immutable cut on its line (FOUNDATIONS §2);\n- release version is a release-process fact; and\n- release version is not the live constitutional identity of every\n  object in the project.\n\nSo the method distinguishes object identity, release identity,\npublication or revision coordinates, and mutable discovery selectors.\nThese may relate, but they are not interchangeable (P3, P6).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/IDENTITY.md#IDENTITY%2F9.%20Release%20version%20boundary",
          "start_line":335
        }
      ],
      "key":"release_and_object_coordinates",
      "label":"Release and object identity coordinates"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A surface defining or exposing identity, whose adequacy is tested by seven questions about owning authority, denoted kind, uniqueness scope, lifecycle stability, over-trust in readability, unambiguous parsing of families/members/aliases/revisions, and runtime/domain/release category.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":362,
          "quote":"If those questions cannot be answered, the identity surface is\nunder-defined (P3).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/IDENTITY.md#IDENTITY%2F10.%20Minimum%20questions",
