          "quote":"Because this profile makes an edge traversal the unit of compute, an ODD\nproduct must use that unit of compute to build its own operative framework\nbehaviour. The required order is: declare the outcome\ntraversal over typed assets; publish the declarative GTL/ABG carrier\nstructure; implement only the minimal imperative adapter code that remains\nnecessary (P0, P6).\n\nAn outcome traversal declares the governed movement before\nimplementation: source asset type; target asset type; edge traversal or\ngraph-function name; closure or evaluation obligation; lineage obligation;\nambiguity or gap obligation; allowed `F_D`, `F_P` and `F_H` bindings. The\ndeclarative carrier structure is GTL graph functions, typed nodes,\nvectors, operator bindings, evaluator bindings, carrier schemas, policy\ndeclarations, projection declarations and lineage ledger declarations\n(P0, P3).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/ODD.md#ODD%2F11.%20Core%20law%2F11.2A%20Outcome-first%20realisation%20order",
          "start_line":623
        }
      ],
      "key":"outcome_first_construction_order",
      "statement":"Use edge traversal to build operative framework behaviour, in order: declare outcome traversal over typed assets; publish declarative GTL/ABG carriers; implement only remaining necessary minimal imperative adapters. Before implementation declare source/target types, edge/function name, closure/evaluation, lineage, ambiguity/gap obligations and allowed F_D/F_P/F_H bindings. Declarative structure comprises graph functions, typed nodes/vectors, operator/evaluator bindings, carrier schemas, policy/projection declarations and lineage ledgers. Imperative-first behaviour later described in GTL is ODD-shaped but not yet ODD-built.",
      "uncertainty":""
